        {
          "id": "94800b41-c581-30d2-879d-2f1c72f02423",
          "content": {
            "id": "94800b41-c581-30d2-879d-2f1c72f02423",
            "contentType": "VIDEO",
            "title": "Quicker Boeing deliveries help Ryanair boost profit, traffic forecast",
            "description": "<title><body><p>STORY: Ryanair profits flew up 42% in the first six months of the year.</p><p>The budget airline reported a forecast-beating $2.96 billion on Monday (November 3).</p><p>That was well up from the same period last year and above analyst projections.</p><p>Average fares also grew by 13% year-on-year.</p><p>Ryanair also raised its passenger traffic forecast Monday.</p><p>It cited earlier than expected Boeing deliveries and strong first-half demand.</p><p>The carrier said it cautiously expects to recover all of last year's 7% average fare decline in the financial year to March end.</p><p>It further added this should lead to what it called 'reasonable' full-year net profit growth.</p><p>Ryanair expects to fly 207 million passengers to end-March - up by a million from a previous forecast.</p><p>The uptick comes after the airline received 23 new MAX 8 aircraft from Boeing.</p><p>Ryanair said it is confident of receiving the six remaining MAX 8 aircraft from an order that had suffered long delays by February.</p><p>Shares were down over 2% in early Monday trade.\u00a0</p></body></title>",
            "summary": "<body><p>STORY: Ryanair profits flew up 42% in the first six months of the year.</p><p>The budget airline reported a forecast-beating $2.96 billion on Monday (November 3).</p><p>That was well up from the same period last year and above analyst projections.</p><p>Average fares also grew by 13% year-on-year.</p><p>Ryanair also raised its passenger traffic forecast Monday.</p><p>It cited earlier than expected Boeing deliveries and strong first-half demand.</p><p>The carrier said it cautiously expects to recover all of last year's 7% average fare decline in the financial year to March end.</p><p>It further added this should lead to what it called 'reasonable' full-year net profit growth.</p><p>Ryanair expects to fly 207 million passengers to end-March - up by a million from a previous forecast.</p><p>The uptick comes after the airline received 23 new MAX 8 aircraft from Boeing.</p><p>Ryanair said it is confident of receiving the six remaining MAX 8 aircraft from an order that had suffered long delays by February.</p><p>Shares were down over 2% in early Monday trade.&nbsp;</p></body>",
